  "body": "### Problem\n\nThe Usage page combines the **total attempt count and total duration of a routed request** with **only the final provider/error**. Earlier member failures are not shown in the expanded Usage entry. This makes a failover chain look like repeated retries of the displayed provider/model.\n\nObserved on the official Docker image **0.1.1472**. The request used a model group and the Chat streaming endpoint.\n\n### Concrete example\n\nUsage showed the group, NVIDIA NIM, `400 · 400 · 3 attempts`, approximately 52 seconds, and:\n\n`NVIDIA NIM: Validation: Unsupported parameter(s): reasoning, enable_thinking`\n\nThe corresponding routing JSONL shows **three different members, one attempt each**:\n\n| Attempt | Provider / model | Result | Duration |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | codex / gpt-6.1-sol | 504, first-token timeout | 25,023 ms |\n| 2 | raccoon / sn-deepseek-v4-1-flash-1017 | 504, first-token timeout | 25,037 ms |\n| 3 | nvidia / stepfun-ai/step-5-preview | 400, unsupported parameters | 1,577 ms |\n\nTotal recorded request duration: 51,859 ms. NVIDIA was not attempted three times.\n\nA second request illustrates why model identity matters too: five attempts ended with NVIDIA **z-ai/glm-5.3-flash** returning the same unsupported-parameter error, after a Codex timeout, a NVIDIA **stepfun-ai/step-5-preview** timeout, and two other members returning 429. Showing only the final provider/error hides these distinctions.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nPlease make the complete ordered attempt/failover history accessible from the individual Usage entry, for example in its expanded details or a trace popover:\n\n- Provider and model for every attempted member.\n- Result/status, duration, and failure reason for each attempt.\n- A clear distinction between switching to another member and retrying the same member.\n- Label aggregate attempt counts/duration as belonging to the whole client request, and the displayed final error as the final error.\n\nA compact overview plus expandable details would be sufficient; this request does not require changing routing or retry policy. An aggregate count paired only with the final provider should not imply that this provider was retried that many times.\n\n### Verification\n\nCompared the exported request records with their corresponding persisted routing traces and inspected the actual Usage page. The underlying route data already contains the attempt sequence. Raw exports are omitted because they contain private conversation and tool content.\n\nThere is also a separate problem with the existing “View routing” navigation failing to bring the selected historical trace into view, which I am reporting separately. Even with that navigation fixed, the Usage entry should make the meaning of its aggregate attempt count clear.",
  "closed_by": null,
